is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables/nftables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically on its WAN-facing interface `eth2` (the `r6_r3` link connecting router6 to router3 at 10.50.1.1/10.50.1.2).

**Mechanism:** Router6 carries three DROP rules in its FORWARD chain that match all traffic arriving inbound on `eth2`: one drops TCP, one drops UDP, and one drops ICMP (protocol 0). A single narrow exception allows TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 destined to 172.18.51.1. As a result, any return traffic sent by remote hosts back toward PC15–PC18 (172.18.51.0/24 via 172.18.50.0/24) must traverse eth2 inbound to be forwarded to the LAN — and is silently dropped. The ICMP DROP rule counter already shows 30 packets × 84 bytes = 2520 bytes discarded, matching exactly the diagnostic ICMP echo replies generated during this investigation.

**Localization:** Router6, interface eth2, FORWARD chain. Outbound traffic from PC15–PC18 exits through eth2 without obstruction (no rules match on the outbound direction), so router6 itself can reach all remote subnets. The blockage is asymmetric: only the WAN→LAN forwarding direction is affected.

**Root cause:** Injected iptables/nftables ACL rules on router6 drop all protocols (ICMP, TCP, UDP) entering from the WAN interface eth2, blocking every return flow destined for the 172.18.51.0/24 subnet and rendering PC15–PC18 unable to complete any bidirectional connection to the rest of the network.

**Fix:** Remove the three DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain on eth2 (`iptables -D FORWARD` for each DROP rule matching `-i eth2`, and flush the corresponding nftables FORWARD rules), restoring symmetric forwarding between the WAN and LAN segments.
=== END ===